Z||000000||BOILERMAKER||01-12-2000||07:36 AM||||OK, this may be a stupid question, but I need to ask it anyway.<P>Here is what I thik, but I want to make sure.  The #1 cylinder on a 2.8 (well, 3.1 in my case) is the rear one closes to the right rear of the car right?  What I mean, just to clarify myself, is that when you are standing at the rear bumper (looking at the engine) of the car the #1 cylinder is the one closest to you on the far right.<P>Then, do the rest go as follows?<P>6 4 2<BR>5 3 1<P>Stupid question, and I should already know this, but I want to make sure.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Wolfhound||01-12-2000||08:07 AM||||Correct!||||reg||1
Z||000002||dogtired||01-12-2000||12:40 PM||||YES   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>YES   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>and YES  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by dogtired (edited 01-12-2000).]||||reg||6
Z||000003||Wolfhound||01-12-2000||09:44 AM||||Always been told the only stupid question is the one you don't ask!||||reg||7
Z||000004||BOILERMAKER||01-12-2000||11:09 AM||||I agree, but I felt like I should have known that information before I asked (wait, ended up that I did, just wasn't sure).<P>Anyway, just put in my new distributer last night and marked where the #1 cylinder will hit and tightened down the distributer clamp, but the directions said not to fully tighten down the clamp for timing purposes.  Can someone tell me a little more information on what I will be doing and tweaking on the distributer base when the time comes to dial this engine in?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Wolfhound||01-12-2000||12:46 PM||||Have the clamp snug but still where you can turn it by hand. With the ECM disabled attach the timing light to the no. one plug wire. rotate distributor clockwise to advance the timing mark and counter clockwise to retard.  <p>[This message has been edited by Wolfhound (edited 01-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||DRH||01-12-2000||01:21 PM||||Your question makes me think you have not done this before???<P>How much info do you need? The reason you leave the dist. loose is so you can twist it to set the exact timing with a timing light. It should be tight enough it will not turn on it's own but loose enough you can twist it by hand.<P>Tip on setting initial timing a little more precisely than trying to line up rotor with cap: After aligning #1 (as you have done), rotate engine (manually, too precise for starter) until appropriate timing mark (10 deg) is aligned on front pulley. You will need to go past 10 and come back to make sure the last direction the crank turns is from 10 toward 0. This will take up any slack in timing chain, dist gear, etc. Now, with dist. cap off look at the 6 pointed "star" that rotates with the shaft. The points should be very close to lining up with the points on the stationary piece. Rotate the dist. slightly to get an EXACT alignment. This should put your timing VERY close. My experience has been that it is usually within a couple of degrees. <P><p>[This message has been edited by DRH (edited 01-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||BOILERMAKER||01-12-2000||01:43 PM||||As far as having done this before, no I have not.  I figured from what I have read that the timing had something like the in-depth instruction mentioned.  I just didn't know the specifics of what to do.  Thanks for all the help.  I still have to do the Turbo portion of the engine first, but I am getting as much information as possible before all of the big jobs hit.||||reg||1
